[[Governor of New Jersey|Governor]] [[Phil Murphy]] was elected to a second term in [[2021 New Jersey gubernatorial election|2021]] with 51.2% of the vote. He was [[Term limits in the United States|term-limited]] by the [[New Jersey Constitution]] and couldn't seek re-election for a third consecutive term. [[Jersey City, New Jersey|Jersey City]] [[Mayor of Jersey City, New Jersey|mayor]] [[Steven Fulop]], former [[New Jersey Senate]] president [[Stephen Sweeney]], U.S. Representatives [[Josh Gottheimer]] and [[Mikie Sherrill]], and [[Newark, New Jersey|Newark]] [[Mayor of Newark, New Jersey|mayor]] [[Ras Baraka]] ran for the Democratic nomination.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://newjerseyglobe.com/governor/fulop-announces-bid-for-governor-in-2025/|title=Fulop announces bid for Governor in 2025|last=Wildstein|first=David|date=April 11, 2023|access-date=April 11, 2023|work=[[New Jersey Globe]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.politico.com/news/2023/12/11/steve-sweeney-new-jersey-governors-race-00131060|title=Sweeney enters NJ governor’s race after upset loss as Senate president|last=Racioppi|first=Dustin|date=December 11, 2023|access-date=December 11, 2023|work=Politico}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://newjerseyglobe.com/governor/baraka-says-hell-run-for-governor-in-2025/|title=Baraka Says He’ll Run For Governor In 2025|last=Wildstein|first=David|date=February 19, 2024|access-date=February 19, 2024|work=[[New Jersey Globe]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Rep. Josh Gottheimer makes it official: He's running for governor of New Jersey|url=https://www.northjersey.com/story/news/politics/elections/2024/11/15/josh-gottheimer-nj-governor-2025-race-election/76291264007/|last=Sobko|first=Katie|date=November 15, 2024|access-date=November 15, 2024|work=[[North Jersey]]}}</ref><ref name="Sherrill">{{cite news |title=New Jersey Rep. Mikie Sherrill’s campaign for governor takes flight |first=Matt |last=Friedman |work=[[Politico]] |date=November 18, 2024 |access-date=November 18, 2024 |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2024/11/18/sherrill-2025-campaign-takes-off-00190108}}</ref> Ultimately, Sherrill won the Democratic nomination on June 10. Former state Assemblyman [[Jack Ciattarelli]], who was the Republican nominee in [[2021 New Jersey gubernatorial election|2021]], was the Republican nominee.

Sherrill won the election, becoming the second female governor of New Jersey when several media outlets called the race for her over Ciattarelli on November 4, 2025.<ref name=":0">{{Cite news |last=Fernandez |first=Madison |date=November 4, 2025 |title=Sherrill Wins NJ gov |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2025/11/04/mikie-sherrill-new-jersey-governor-wins-00635466 |url-status=live |access-date=November 17, 2025 |work=[[Politico]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=November 4, 2025 |title=AP Race Call: Democrat Mikie Sherrill elected NJ governor over Trump-backed Jack Ciattarelli |url=https://apnews.com/article/new-jersey-nj-governor-bf74d0bf8607149b54930b3aff6236bb |url-status=live |access-date=November 17, 2025 |work=[[Associated Press]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Saenz |first=Arlette |date=November 4, 2025 |title=Democrat Mikie Sherrill wins New Jersey governor’s race |url=https://www.cnn.com/2025/11/04/politics/nj-governor-mikie-sherrill-wins |url-status=live |access-date=November 17, 2025 |work=[[CNN]]}}</ref> In addition, she won three consecutive terms for her party for the first time in the state since 1961.<ref name=":0" /> Sherrill outperformed expectations after Republicans outperformed in [[2021 New Jersey gubernatorial election|2021]] & [[2024 United States presidential election in New Jersey|2024]], winning by approximately 14 points against Ciattarelli.<ref>{{Cite news |date=November 4, 2025 |title=New Jersey governor election results |url=https://www.cnn.com/election/2025/results/new-jersey |url-status=live |access-date=November 17, 2025 |work=[[CNN]]}}</ref>

