[[Governor of Virginia|Governor]] [[Glenn Youngkin]] was elected in [[2021 Virginia gubernatorial election|2021]] with 50.6% of the vote. He was [[Term limits in the United States|term-limited]] by the [[Virginia Constitution]] and could not seek election to a second consecutive term. [[Lieutenant Governor of Virginia|Lieutenant Governor]] [[Winsome Earle-Sears]] was the Republican nominee.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Nadeau |first=Ryan |date=2025-04-05 |title=Earle-Sears secures Republican nomination for Virginia governor, creating historic gubernatorial ticket |url=https://www.wric.com/news/politics/local-election-hq/earle-sears-republican-nomination-virginia-governor/ |access-date=2025-04-05 |work=WIRC}}</ref> Former [[United States House of Representatives|U.S. Representative]] for [[Virginia's 7th congressional district]] [[Abigail Spanberger]] was the Democratic nominee.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Fortinsky |first=Sarah |date=2025-04-03 |title=Spanberger confirmed as Democratic nominee for Virginia governor’s race |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/5231525-spanberger-confirmed-as-democratic-nominee-for-virginia-governors-race/amp/ |access-date=2025-04-03 |work=The Hill}}</ref> Both candidates won their respective party’s nominations unopposed. Spanberger won the election by over 15 points, becoming the first [[List of female governors in the United States|female governor]] in Virginia's history.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2025-11-05 |title=Abigail Spanberger Flips Virginia, Becomes State’s First Female Governor |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/11/05/us/abigail-spanberger-virginia-governor-election.html |access-date=2025-11-05 |language=en}}</ref>

